# TODO — Notification Service

Day-sized milestones, in build order. Each one ends in something you can
actually run and see. Source of truth: `docs/dd.md`.

## Done
- [x] Solution layout: `src/`, `tests/`, `docs/`
- [x] Domain model: `Notification` + `NotificationStatus` with state transitions
- [x] Persistence: EF Core (SQLite) `AppDbContext` + initial migration
- [x] Application interfaces: `INotificationService`, `INotificationRepository`,
      `IRepository`, `IUnitOfWork`, `IQueue`
- [x] `NotificationService` (create/get/update/delete)
- [x] Infrastructure: repositories, `UnitOfWork`, `InMemoryQueue`, DI wiring
- [x] Central error handling: `GlobalExceptionHandler` + ProblemDetails + HTTP logging
- [x] Automatic migrations on startup (Development)

---

## M1 — Notification intake API
Turn the API into a real accept-and-inspect surface.

- `POST /notifications`: validate, create, store, publish, return `202 Accepted`
- `GET /notifications/{id}` to expose current status
- Consistent ProblemDetails for validation/bad input
- Remove the `/hello` and `/exception` placeholders
- EF entity configuration + indexes for status/time (with migration)

**Done when:** posting a valid notification returns `202` with an id, the row is
stored as `Queued`, `GET` returns it, and invalid input returns problem details.

---

## M2 — Delivery pipeline, happy path
Make queued work actually get delivered, behind a provider boundary.

- `INotificationProvider` abstraction + fake email provider (can simulate failure)
- Hosted background worker that consumes the queue
- `Queued → Processing → Sent` transitions; skip notifications already `Sent`
- Structured logging for queued / processing / delivered

**Done when:** after posting, the worker delivers it and the status becomes
`Sent`; re-delivering an already-`Sent` notification is a no-op.

---

## M3 — Local infrastructure & configuration
Make the system runnable together with its real dependencies.

- `docker-compose.yml` with SQL DB and RabbitMQ
- DB and RabbitMQ connection details from configuration, not hardcoded

**Done when:** from a clean checkout, `docker compose up` starts the
dependencies and the app connects to them.

---

## M4 — Durable messaging with RabbitMQ
Replace the in-memory queue with the real message boundary.

- RabbitMQ-backed `IQueue<Notification>`, publishing only the notification id
- Exchange + queue setup; explicit ack only after successful processing
- At-least-once delivery; consumer tolerates duplicates

**Done when:** messages flow through RabbitMQ; killing the worker mid-flight and
restarting it still processes the pending work.

---

## M5 — Retries & dead-lettering
Handle the failure path described in dd.md.

- Exponential backoff retries (1s, 2s, 4s …) with `RetryCount` tracked
- Dead-letter queue for notifications that exhaust retries
- Failure recorded on the notification; duplicate-safe retry handling

**Done when:** forcing provider failure shows retries then dead-lettering, and
the database reflects `Failed`.

---

## M6 — Test suite & DoD verification
Lock in the behaviour and confirm the end-to-end flow.

- Test project(s) under `tests/` added to the solution
- Cover the dd.md §13 scenarios: accepted/stored, published, delivered, retry on
  failure, retry exhaustion → DLQ, already-sent not re-delivered, status retrievable
- Verify the full happy and failure paths end to end

**Done when:** `dotnet test` is green and both `Queued → Processing → Sent` and
`Queued → Processing → Failed → Retry → Failed/DLQ` are demonstrated.

---

## After the core (dd.md §14)
Not daily milestones — revisit once the DoD flow works.

- Outbox pattern for DB/publish consistency
- Additional channels (SMS, push)
- Scheduled notifications
- Priority handling
- Metrics/monitoring (delivery rate, retries, failures, latency)
- Authentication and authorization
